---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RansomNoteComposer from '../components/RansomNoteComposer.astro';

const site = Astro.site?.href ?? 'https://88cutouts.com/';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How do I make ransom note text on my phone?',
    a: 'Tap one of the phrases above or type your own, then tap Copy. The letters are rendered as a transparent PNG, so you can paste it straight into an Instagram Story, a chat or a note. Nothing is installed and there is no account.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which letters and symbols are available?',
    a: 'Plain A–Z in either case (it is not case-sensitive, since every cutout is its own shape anyway), 0–9, and the punctuation . , ! ? " \' - : ; ( ) @ # % + * & >. Emoji and accented characters have no matching paper scrap, so they are skipped.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does the same word look different every time?',
    a: 'Each letter is picked at random from a small pool of scrap variants and given a fresh tilt and vertical offset. That is deliberate — a real cut-out note never repeats exactly. Use Shuffle the cut if you want a different arrangement of the same message.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the opacity slider for?',
    a: 'It makes the lettering see-through. At around 20–45% the text sits over a photo like a watermark instead of covering it, which is the usual reason people want translucent text on a Story. The Copy and Download output matches whatever the preview shows.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is the image free to use?',
    a: 'Yes — free for personal use on your own Stories, chats and notes, with no watermark and no attribution required. The same terms as the rest of the sticker library.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does anything I type get uploaded?',
    a: 'No. The letters are drawn onto a canvas inside your own browser and the finished PNG never leaves your device. There is no server-side rendering step and nothing is logged.',
  },
];

const jsonld = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebApplication',
    name: 'Cutout Text Maker (Ransom Note Generator)',
    url: new URL('/ransom-note/', site).href,
    applicationCategory: 'DesignApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'Any',
    browserRequirements: 'Requires JavaScript',
    description:
      'Type a message and get it back as a cut-out ransom-note collage you can copy straight into an Instagram Story, at any opacity.',
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
    featureList: [
      'One-tap example phrases',
      'Live transparent PNG preview',
      'Adjustable opacity for translucent lettering',
      'Three letter sizes',
      'Copy to clipboard or download',
    ],
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
    })),
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'HowTo',
    name: 'How to make cut-out ransom note text for an Instagram Story',
    totalTime: 'PT1M',
    step: [
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        name: 'Choose a phrase',
        text: 'Tap one of the example phrases, or type your own message of up to 140 characters.',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        name: 'Adjust the look',
        text: 'Shuffle the cut for a different arrangement, pick a letter size, and lower the opacity if you want the text to be see-through.',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        name: 'Copy and paste',
        text: 'Tap Copy, open Instagram, start a Story and long-press to paste the transparent PNG.',
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Cutout text maker — ransom note generator for Stories | 88cutouts"
  description="Type anything and get it back as magazine-cutout ransom note letters. Tap a phrase, set the opacity, then copy the transparent PNG straight into your Instagram Story. Free, no account."
  jsonld={jsonld}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="crumbs">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span>Cutout text
    </p>

    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Cutout text maker</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="lede">
      Every letter is a real photo of a paper scrap, tilted and jittered like it was snipped
      out of a magazine. Tap a phrase below to see it work, then make it your own — it takes
      about ten seconds and hands you a transparent PNG.
    </p>

    <RansomNoteComposer />

    <article class="prose">
      <h2>What to use it for</h2>
      <p>
        Cut-out lettering does one job extremely well: it makes a short line of text look
        like an object rather than a caption. Instagram's built-in fonts are clean and
        uniform, which is exactly why they disappear — everyone has the same ones. A paper
        letter has a shadow, a torn edge and a slight tilt, so it reads as something placed
        on the photo instead of typed over it.
      </p>
      <p>The messages that work best are the short ones:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A date or a number.</strong> "2026", a birthday, a countdown. Numerals are
          the strongest characters in the set because they are wide and unambiguous.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A name or a nickname.</strong> One word, large, in the corner of a photo
          dump — it labels the Story without a caption bar covering the image.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A two-word reaction.</strong> "miss you", "my era", "no thoughts". The
          register of cut-out type is slightly deadpan, which is why it suits these.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>An announcement.</strong> "save the date", "we're moving", "he said yes".
          The style carries a little urgency, inherited from where it came from.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Long sentences are the one thing to avoid. The canvas wraps them, but every extra
        character shrinks the letters once the image is scaled into a Story, and the paper
        texture — the entire point — is the first thing lost at small sizes. If you need a
        full sentence, split it across two Stories or set it in Instagram's own text and
        keep the cut-out for the headline.
      </p>

      <h2>Making it translucent</h2>
      <p>
        The opacity slider is the difference between text that sits <em>on</em> a photo and
        text that sits <em>over</em> it. At 100% the letters are solid and read as the main
        subject. Around 70% they soften enough to let a busy photo through without losing
        legibility. Below 45% the lettering behaves like a watermark — visible, but clearly
        secondary to the image underneath.
      </p>
      <p>
        The transparency is baked into the PNG itself, not applied by Instagram, so what you
        see in the preview is exactly what you paste. If you plan to layer a translucent
        line over a dark photo, go a little higher than you think — anything under about 20%
        tends to vanish once Instagram compresses the Story.
      </p>

      <h2>How the letters are made</h2>
      <p>
        Every character in the set is a real photograph of a cut-out paper scrap, snipped by
        hand, shot against a plain background and masked to a transparent PNG. There are
        several variants of each letter, so a word with a repeated character does not show
        the same scrap twice in a row. When you type, each character is swapped for one of
        its variants and given a small random rotation and vertical offset — the same way a
        real collage never lines up perfectly.
      </p>
      <p>
        The constraint that makes it look assembled rather than random is that the rotation
        and offset stay inside believable bands. Letters pasted by a human hand are crooked
        by a few degrees, not spun to forty-five. There is more on where the style comes
        from, and how to compose one by hand, in
        <a href="/blog/ransom-note-aesthetic-guide/">the ransom note aesthetic guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Pairing it with stickers</h2>
      <p>
        Text alone on a plain photo can look unfinished. One or two cutouts around it — a
        bow at the corner, a strip of washi tape along the top edge, a heart between two
        words — give the lettering something to belong to, because they share the same
        paper logic. The <a href="/category/coquette/">coquette</a> and
        <a href="/category/hearts/">hearts</a> sets are the usual pairing; the
        <a href="/category/y2k/">y2k</a> set suits the punkier end of it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Keep the palette narrow. Cut-out type is already visually busy, so two or three
        colours across the whole Story is plenty — the same logic as
        <a href="/blog/build-a-cohesive-instagram-aesthetic/">building a cohesive aesthetic</a>
        across a grid.
      </p>

      <h2>Pasting it into a Story</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Copy</strong> puts the PNG on your clipboard. Open Instagram, start a Story,
        tap once on the screen and long-press to paste — it drops in as a sticker you can
        pinch, rotate and place, with no white box around it. If your browser refuses to
        copy images (some older mobile browsers do), <strong>Download</strong> saves the
        same file so you can add it from your camera roll instead.
      </p>
      <p>
        The full walkthrough, including WhatsApp and the cases where transparency gets
        flattened, is in
        <a href="/blog/how-to-add-stickers-to-instagram-stories/">how to add stickers to Instagram Stories</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>FAQ</h2>
      {
        faq.map((f) => (
          <>
            <h3>{f.q}</h3>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </>
        ))
      }
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
